.Dd June 6, 2026
.Dt KLDMGRD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldmgrd
.Nd kernel module loading manager
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a service managed by
.Xr serviced 8
that provides controlled kernel module loading and unloading.
It receives system gate tokens for
.Dq kldload
and
.Dq kldunload
from the Oracle and exposes them to authorized clients via the
naming registry as
.Dq org.5bsd.system.kldmgr .
.Pp
On profiles where the kldload and kldunload system gates are not
claimed (e.g., the desktop profile),
.Nm
starts but has no tokens.
Processes can load modules directly via
.Xr kldload 2
without going through
.Nm .
.Pp
On server and embedded profiles, the gates are claimed by the
Oracle and
.Nm
is the only path to load or unload kernel modules.
.Sh PROTOCOL
Clients connect via
.Fn service_lookup "org.5bsd.system.kldmgr"
and exchange messages over the resulting pair fd.
.Pp
All requests use
.Vt struct kldmgr_req
with the following operation codes:
.Bl -tag -width KLDMGR_OP_UNLOAD
.It Dv KLDMGR_OP_LOAD Pq 1
Load the kernel module named in
.Fa name .
Returns
.Vt struct kldmgr_reply
with the module id on success.
.It Dv KLDMGR_OP_UNLOAD Pq 2
Unload the kernel module named in
.Fa name .
Returns
.Vt struct kldmgr_reply .
.It Dv KLDMGR_OP_LIST Pq 3
List all loaded kernel modules.
Returns
.Vt struct kldmgr_list_reply
with a count and array of entries.
.El
.Sh MANIFEST
A typical manifest for
.Nm :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bundle_id = "org.5bsd.systemcontrol";
program = "kldmgrd";
provides = ["org.5bsd.system.kldmgr"];
restart = "on-failure";
capabilities {
    system = ["kldload", "kldunload"];
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kldload 2 ,
.Xr kldunload 2 ,
.Xr kldstat 2 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr oracled 8 ,
.Xr serviced 8
